  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three kinds of helical macromolecular systems by how easily their handedness changes and how chirality can be induced. On the left, a static helical polymer is shown as either a left- or right-handed helix, with a high inversion barrier, meaning each helix sense is stable and does not readily switch. Several example polymer structures are sketched underneath.\n\nIn the middle, a dynamic helical polymer has a low inversion barrier. Its helix can locally invert, illustrated by a segment where the handedness changes along the chain, implying a more flexible and reversible helical state. Example repeat-unit structures are again shown below.\n\nOn the right, a foldamer is depicted as switching between a non-helical conformation and helical forms of opposite handedness. A chiral agent appears to bind to the helical foldamer and bias or stabilize one screw sense. This emphasizes that foldamers can adopt helicity through folding rather than being permanently locked into a helix, and that external chiral information can control the resulting handedness.\n\nOverall, the figure highlights a progression from rigid, fixed helices to dynamic helices and then to foldable systems whose helicity is induced and selected by molecular interactions.",
